 "engine": "elevenlabs:scribe_v1",
 "text": "Imagine buying your first house and six months later, boom, five thousand dollars for a new AC. The first thing our parents be telling us as soon as we graduate and start working is, \"You need to stop throwing your money away on rent and start saving to buy a house.\" That's the first thing they be telling us. They don't tell us, \"Hey, open a Roth IRA and start investing.\" They don't teach us about credit or how to manage our money, but they gon' tell you, \"You need to stop renting and buy that house.\" Can I learn how to budget my first paycheck before y'all put me on a thirty-year payment plan? So boom, you get your credit right, the bank approves you, and you finally get the keys. Six months later, in the middle of July, your AC goes out. If you're renting and that repair is on your landlord, you call them to get it fixed. If you own the house, you call the AC company, and that five thousand dollar bill has your name on it. And if buying the house wiped out your savings, man, you're cooked, in Florida with no AC. Then somebody says, \"But you're building equity.\" And they're right. Equity is what your house is worth minus what you still owe. But early on, a lot of your mortgage payment can go to interest. So if you buy for three hundred grand and sell for four hundred, you still gotta count the interest, repairs, and fees before you call that whole hundred grand profit. And even after you pay off the mortgage, the property tax bill still comes. In Florida, leave those taxes unpaid long enough, and your house can actually be auctioned off. Me personally, I'd rather rent right now, stack my money, invest some of it, and give myself room to move. If buying is your goal, do the math. Do you actually want to stay in that neighborhood? And after you get the keys, can you still afford what breaks? Be real. If you had the down payment today, are you buying or stacking that money a little longer?",
